tac_plus-ng tests
=================

Functional tests for tac_plus-ng: the daemon runs under spawnd on a
loopback port, as in production, and Python clients talk TACACS+ to it.
Only the Python 3 standard library is needed (3.8 or newer).

The tests are written for Linux. Elsewhere they run, but the checks
that look at processes through /proc (dead workers, sockets a worker
keeps open, MAVIS children left behind) are skipped, and so are the
self-tests of those checks; run-tests uses gmake if there is one.

Quick start on a fresh Ubuntu:

    sudo apt-get install build-essential perl libpcre2-dev libssl-dev \
        libcrypt-dev python3 openssl
    tac_plus-ng/tests/run-tests

The openssl command makes the certificates of the TLS tests (in their
scratch directories; none are kept in the tree) and is the TLS-PSK client;
without it those tests are skipped.

run-tests configures and builds tac_plus-ng, installs it into
build/<OS>/tests-prefix (no root needed, nothing outside the tree is
touched) and runs every test. Arguments after the options go to
"python3 -m unittest", e.g.

    tac_plus-ng/tests/run-tests -k pap         # tests with "pap" in the name
    tac_plus-ng/tests/run-tests --build-only   # build and install only

To test an existing installation, or a build of another tree:

    TACTEST_PREFIX=/usr/local tac_plus-ng/tests/run-tests

To build with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer (configure
keeps only the compiler name, so run-tests passes the flags again to
make; llvm puts llvm-symbolizer, for the stack traces in the reports,
on PATH):

    sudo apt-get install clang llvm
    TACTEST_CC="clang -g -fsanitize=address,undefined \
        -fsanitize-ignorelist=$PWD/tac_plus-ng/tests/sanitizer-ignorelist.txt" \
        tac_plus-ng/tests/run-tests

sanitizer-ignorelist.txt (compile time) and ubsan.supp (run time) list
the findings that are known and not fixed yet, each with the reason;
test_harness.py checks that every ubsan.supp line is still needed.
Changing TACTEST_CC or TACTEST_CONFIGURE rebuilds from scratch. On
kernels with high mmap randomization, ASan binaries of older clang
versions crash at start; "setarch $(uname -m) -R tac_plus-ng/tests/run-tests"
or "sysctl vm.mmap_rnd_bits=28" helps.

Other environment variables: TACTEST_CONFIGURE (extra configure
arguments), TACTEST_WORKDIR, TACTEST_KEEP=1, TACTEST_DEBUG=<mask>,
TACTEST_TIMEOUT (seconds, default 600).

Layout
------

    run-tests        build + install + run, the one entry point
    tactest/         the harness
      daemon.py        start/stop/reload the daemon, process discovery
      detect.py        worker crash detectors, checked after every test
      config.py        the configuration template around a test's policy
      tacacs.py        TACACS+ client (RFC 8907): ASCII login, PAP, CHAP,
                       MS-CHAP, enable, authorization, accounting; raw and
                       split packets; several sessions on one connection;
                       over TLS (RFC 9887); PROXY protocol v1/v2 headers
      tls.py           a throw-away CA (openssl command), client contexts,
                       TLS 1.3 PSK through "openssl s_client"
      crypto.py        MD4 and DES for MS-CHAP (not in the standard library)
      radius.py        RADIUS client: PAP Access-Request over UDP, TCP or
                       TLS; Message-Authenticator in requests and replies
      mavis_stub.py    a MAVIS backend for the "external" module, on top of
                       the installed mavis.py; tests change its answers
                       through a JSON file while the daemon runs
    configs/         complete configurations for "tac_plus-ng -P":
                     good-*.cfg must parse, bad-*.cfg must be rejected
    test_*.py        the tests, one file per topic

Writing a test
--------------

Derive from tactest.DaemonTestCase and write the policy into the class:

    class Example(DaemonTestCase):
        CONFIG = """
            user alice { password pap = clear secret }
            ruleset { rule { script { permit } } }
        """

        def test_pap(self):
            self.assertTrue(self.connect().pap("alice", "secret").ok)

The daemon is started once per class. After every test the harness
checks that no worker crashed, hung or kept a socket open -- spawnd
restarts a crashed worker, so a crash is otherwise invisible to the
client. A test for an issue is named test_issue<N>_<what>, so that
"run-tests -k issue<N>" selects it.

A test that needs a daemon of its own -- a configuration the daemon
under test may not accept, a reload -- sets CONFIG = None and calls
self.start_daemon(policy); the same checks run when the test ends.
self.wait_log(name, text) looks only at what was logged during the test.
A class with UDP = True also gets a RADIUS listener (self.radius(secret)).
LISTEN = {name: options} adds spawnd listeners with ports of their own,
e.g. {"tls": "tls = yes"}; self.connect(listener="tls", tls=context).

For users from a MAVIS backend, put tactest.Stub.module() into the
policy and drive the backend from the test:

    class Backend(DaemonTestCase):
        CONFIG = Stub.module(childs=(1, 1)) + """
            user backend = mavis
            pap backend = mavis
            ruleset { rule { script { permit } } }
        """

        def test_error(self):
            stub = Stub(self.daemon)
            stub.set(users={"alice": {"password": "pw"}}, result={"AUTH": "ERROR"})
            self.assertEqual(self.connect().pap("alice", "pw").status_name, "FAIL")
            self.assertEqual(stub.requests()[-1]["result"], "ERROR")

The policy is a template like the rest of the configuration: "$tmp" is
the daemon's scratch directory, a literal "$" is written "$$".

A regression test goes into the same change as its fix, not before it
as an expectedFailure: the fix would turn that into an unexpected
success, and expectedFailure doesn't cover the checks that run after
the test anyway.

Scratch directories (configuration, logs, sanitizer reports) of failed
test classes are kept under build/tests-work and their paths printed.

